1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,920
Okay, I'm just going to take you through some of the changes that we're making to the BurpSuite

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,040
inspector. So first of all, what is the inspector? It's basically a collapsible side panel that's

3
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:24,580
available in the message editor next to request and response views, and it provides a way to

4
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:31,620
analyze and manipulate messages that you send. So just to be clear, we're talking about this bit of the

5
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:38,740
interface. So what does it do? It allows you to view and edit request data in tables. So we can edit them

6
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:46,060
inline at this top level view. And we can also go into a drill down view to see more data. It offers inline

7
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:51,680
decoding features. So these appear when you select text in the editor. And once you go in the drill down

8
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:57,240
view, if you have an encoded value there, it'll automatically decode it for you. And it's an area

9
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:03,260
where we're going to utilize for future tools of wizardry. One such feature lately being the tool

10
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:10,820
that we introduced for James's HTTP2 research. So what's happening with the inspector? Before we

11
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:16,420
released it, we had some assumptions on what the reception would be like. We knew it would be very

12
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:22,420
varied. And it would be like Marmite. Some people love it. Some people hate it. And this is because it

13
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:28,580
introduced a new workflow for users by removing tabs that they previously used into the contextual

14
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:35,740
side panel. Users don't like change. We know that pretty much. And we felt important features were

15
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:41,380
missing from the MVP, such as the ability to resize it, to be able to dock it to the left and so on.

16
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:47,980
So once we released the inspector, we had some great feedback on Twitter. People were loving the

17
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:54,400
decoding features that we added to it and stating that we made their lives easier. But on the opposite

18
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:59,800
spectrum of that, people missed the old headers and hex tabs. They wanted the inspector to be

19
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:06,400
resizable and they'd rather have it on the left. The best tweet that we received was in response to

20
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:13,720
DAF's video of the new office, which juxtaposed the new office with the repeater UI showing the request,

21
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:19,900
the response, and the inspector lobbed onto the side. Even better was DAF's reply saying,

22
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:24,580
that looks fantastic. We'll get the builders in. So I think planning permission is going ahead for that.

23
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:32,980
So following the launch, we reached out to the Twitter rebels. We conducted interviews with some of

24
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:41,920
those individuals. We did a survey. And we collated all of that along with telemetry to form the strategy

25
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:48,040
that we were going to move forward with. So the key insights that we gained from the feedback was that

26
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:54,640
the inspector was too far away. On the right, you can see a screenshot which was from user feedback,

27
00:02:54,640 --> 00:03:01,060
and they felt the need to add a banana to it for scale. For some users, if they had the inspector

28
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:07,000
closed, it would be one to two extra clicks. So one click to open the inspector, a second click to open

29
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,980
the widget in which they wanted to view. Some users just didn't have the screen real estate for it.

30
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:19,660
We've got users on very small laptops, as it turns out. Other users just wanted the tabs back. And

31
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:26,740
users also wanted more features such as being able to decode JSON web tokens. So I'll just run you

32
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:32,140
through a prototype that addresses a lot of those changes and helps speed up the user workflow. So

33
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:36,940
if I open the inspector, we've made some immediate changes to the interface, just simple things like

34
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:42,700
grouping counters so that there's a consistent area for those. At the top, we've got some inline

35
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:48,880
options so that we can dot the inspector to the left or to the right. We've got an expand all feature,

36
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:54,820
which opens all the widgets that contain data. So as you can see here, if we close the inspector

37
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:58,360
and open it, we're starting to get to a state where the workflow is faster and we haven't got to keep

38
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:04,720
opening widgets all the time. We've got a collapse all. And we've also introduced a wrapping feature

39
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:10,780
at the widget level so that cells are wrapped. And that means that it's easier to access and edit the

40
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:16,360
data with an inline feature that we've already got live. And so we're starting to get somewhere with

41
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:22,900
this. But we can do more things to help users with their workflow. So we've introduced a configuration

42
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:28,720
dialogue where users can select the widgets and reorder them in the order that they'd like. We can

43
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:34,360
choose whether or not to show or hide the widgets. Widgets can be opened by default so that we don't have

44
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:41,080
to manually open them. And we can set which widgets we want to wrap text by default. So imagine we want

45
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:47,020
to set request cookies to be always open. Let's hide response headers and let's set all of the widgets to

46
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:52,900
wrap. We can choose the position of the inspector. So let's dock it to the left and let's set it so that

47
00:04:52,900 --> 00:05:01,120
it's always closed by default. So when I next open a tab or send a request into repeater, this will be my default view.

48
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:07,660
And when I open it, I'll get the request cookies widget open and the cell data will automatically be wrapped.

49
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:17,120
But we could still do more. So we can also take the available widgets in the inspector and also add them back in as tabs in the editor,

50
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:22,760
which is where the real power of this comes in as the user can start to configure their own workspace.

51
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:27,740
So we can turn on headers, params, cookies and attributes and save that.

52
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:32,000
As you can see, they now appear in the message editor.

53
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:37,700
The advantage of this is that we're using the inspector widgets themselves rather than going back to the old view,

54
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:43,040
meaning that we can auto wrap cell data. They have the decoding features at the drill down view.

55
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:48,800
And you can also view the view as a table with the inspector open with the inline decoding available.

56
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:56,900
So what we've done is we've allowed the user to configure their workspace so that all the workflow is available within one banana.

57
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:03,200
So the work for this is due to go live in a week or two for phase one.

58
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:07,900
The guys are working really hard on it and we hope this is going to make a good impact.

59
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:43,020
Thank you.

